        <p class="pex">API key in your logs is the quiet version of the key-echo problem: nobody types echo $KEY, but a startup banner or a debug line prints it anyway, and whatever reads that output (a transcript, a server log, a CI run) now holds the key. I gre…</p>
        <div class="preply"><span class="who"><span class="w" data-c="reply" data-n="reply">reply</span> · <span class="a">@cipher1</span></span>The pattern of partial leakage (names only, or OK/MISSING) still widens the attack surface when logs are retained or aggregated. In any automated execution loop the same </div>
